---
import Legal from '../layouts/Legal.astro';
import { REPO } from '../lib/releases.js';

const CONTACT = 'qrate@dvnl.work';
---

<Legal
  title="Privacy policy"
  eyebrow="Privacy"
  updated="15 August 2026"
  description="qrate is a desktop app with no accounts, no telemetry and no analytics. Your collection stays on your own disk. What it touches, and what it never sends."
  lede="qrate is a desktop application. Your catalogue stays on your own disk, and the project operates no servers that hold your data."
>
  <h2>The short version</h2>
  <p>
    qrate has no accounts, no telemetry and no analytics. It does not phone home. Every record,
    field, note and linked file stays in the <code>.qrate</code> file on your own disk. The only time
    qrate talks to anyone else is when you ask it to: exporting to Google Sheets, downloading a
    dictionary, or checking for a release.
  </p>

  <h2>What qrate collects</h2>
  <p>
    Nothing. The application does not send your catalogue, your file paths, your settings or any
    usage data to us or to anyone else. There is no crash reporting service and no usage counter. A
    session log is written on your own machine to help you diagnose problems; it stays there unless
    you choose to attach it to a bug report.
  </p>

  <h2>This website</h2>
  <p>
    This site is static — pages and images, no accounts and no tracking cookies. It is served by
    Cloudflare, which records standard request information such as IP address, user agent and the
    page requested, for delivery and abuse prevention. We do not use that data to build profiles,
    and we run no advertising or analytics scripts. Application downloads are served by GitHub,
    whose own privacy policy applies once you follow a download link.
  </p>
  <p>
    One address on this site answers qrate rather than a browser: it hands the application its own
    Google API credentials, so they can be rotated without shipping a new release. It carries no
    personal data in either direction, and your Google sign-in never passes through it — that
    happens between your browser, Google, and your own computer.
  </p>

  <h2>Google user data</h2>
  <p>
    qrate can export a project to a Google Sheet. That is the only feature that touches a Google
    account, it is entirely optional, and nothing happens until you start it and consent in your
    browser.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Scope requested:</strong> <code>drive.file</code> only. This scope reaches only the
      files qrate itself creates and the ones you hand it by name through Google's own file chooser.
      It gives qrate no access to the rest of your Drive — not your other documents, not your
      folders, not your file list.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Choosing an existing sheet:</strong> qrate opens Google's file chooser in your browser.
      Google shows you your own files and tells qrate about the one you pick, and nothing else. The
      chooser page on this site never sees the list; your selection is passed back to qrate on your
      own machine.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>What is sent:</strong> the rows and columns of the project you chose to export, sent
      directly from your computer to Google's API in order to create the sheet you asked for.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Where it goes:</strong> straight to Google. The data does not pass through any server
      operated by this project, because there is no such server.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sign-in:</strong> a loopback redirect with PKCE. qrate opens your browser, you consent
      to Google directly, and Google returns the result to a port on your own machine. Your Google
      password is never seen by qrate.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Tokens:</strong> the access and refresh tokens Google issues are kept on your computer
      so you are not asked to consent on every export. They are never transmitted to us.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Retention and deletion:</strong> we hold no copy of your Google data, so there is
      nothing for us to delete. Removing qrate's access from your Google account, or deleting the
      local qrate data directory, ends it completely.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    You can withdraw consent at any time from your Google account's <a
      href="https://myaccount.google.com/permissions"
      rel="noopener">third-party access page</a
    >. Sheets qrate has already created stay in your Drive and remain yours.
  </p>

  <h2>Limited Use</h2>
  <div class="callout">
    qrate's use and transfer of information received from Google APIs to any other app will adhere to
    the <a
      href="https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy"
      rel="noopener">Google API Services User Data Policy</a
    >, including the Limited Use requirements.
  </div>
  <p>
    In practice that means Google user data is used only to provide the export you requested. It is
    not sold, not transferred to anyone, not used for advertising, and not read by any human.
  </p>

  <h2>Other things qrate can reach</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Dictionaries.</strong> If you add a spell-check language, qrate downloads that word
      list from its public repository. The request carries no identifying information beyond what any
      download requires.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Plugins.</strong> Plugins run from a folder you control. A plugin you install may
      contact a server you point it at — for example an Islandora site, to check vocabulary terms.
      That connection is yours and is governed by that service's own policy.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Preview tools.</strong> PDFium and ffmpeg run locally. They send nothing anywhere.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Children</h2>
  <p>qrate is a professional cataloguing tool and is not directed at children under 13.</p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    If this policy changes, the date at the top of this page changes with it, and the previous text
    stays in the site's <a href={`https://github.com/${REPO}`} rel="noopener">git history</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Questions about this policy, or about data qrate touches: <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}
      >{CONTACT}</a
    >. See also the <a href="/terms">terms of service</a> and the <a href="/license"
      >licence</a
    >.
  </p>
</Legal>
